# ============================================================================
# Lesson 10.3/10.4/10.5 — Redis: caching, pub/sub, transactions, persistence
# Run in redis-cli.
# ============================================================================

# ---- CACHING (Redis's #1 use — System Design Phase 2) ----
# CACHE-ASIDE pattern (app-side logic, shown as pseudocode):
#   value = GET book:1
#   if value is null:                # cache MISS
#       value = <query Postgres>
#       SET book:1 <value> EX 300    # populate with a 5-min TTL
#   return value
# The TTL bounds staleness; on the next miss it reloads. Cache invalidation on
# write:  DEL book:1  (or SET the new value) when the source row changes.

SET book:1 '{"title":"Clean Code","price":41}' EX 300   # cached JSON with TTL
GET book:1

# ---- EVICTION — Redis is memory-bounded (Phase 7 / System Design 7). Configure
# a maxmemory + policy so it evicts instead of OOMing:
#   CONFIG SET maxmemory 256mb
#   CONFIG SET maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru   # evict least-recently-used keys
# Policies: noeviction (reject writes), allkeys-lru/lfu, volatile-ttl (evict soonest-expiring).
# "A cache without eviction is a memory leak" — Redis makes eviction a config.

# ---- ATOMIC OPERATIONS & rate limiting ----
# Single commands are atomic. A fixed-window rate limiter in 2 commands:
#   INCR rate:user:42            # count this request
#   EXPIRE rate:user:42 60       # window resets after 60s (set on first hit)
#   -> if the value exceeds the limit, reject (429). (Token bucket, System Design 5.)

# ---- TRANSACTIONS (MULTI/EXEC) — queue commands, run atomically ----
MULTI                          # start a transaction
  INCR pageviews
  SET last_view "now"
EXEC                           # run both atomically (no other client interleaves)
# WATCH key -> optimistic locking: abort EXEC if the watched key changed (CAS).
# For complex atomic logic, LUA SCRIPTS (EVAL) run server-side atomically —
# the correct way to do multi-step rate limiting / conditional updates.

# ---- PUB/SUB (fire-and-forget messaging; System Design Phase 9) ----
# Subscriber:   SUBSCRIBE news
# Publisher:    PUBLISH news "hello"    -> all current subscribers get it
# No persistence/replay (unlike Streams/Kafka) — use for live notifications,
# chat fan-out, cache-invalidation broadcasts.

# ---- PIPELINING (throughput) ----
# Send many commands without waiting for each reply (cuts network round-trips).
# In redis-cli you'd batch; in code, use the client's pipeline API.

# ---- PERSISTENCE (Redis can survive restarts despite being in-memory) ----
# RDB : point-in-time SNAPSHOTS to disk (compact, fast restart, some data loss window).
# AOF : append-only log of every write (durable, replayed on restart, larger).
# Use RDB for backups, AOF for durability, or both. Pure cache? Persistence off.
#   CONFIG SET save "900 1"      # RDB snapshot if >=1 change in 900s
#   CONFIG SET appendonly yes    # enable AOF

# ---- CLUSTERING / HA ----
# Redis Sentinel = automatic failover for a primary-replica setup.
# Redis Cluster = sharding across nodes (hash slots) for horizontal scale.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redis + Spring (Spring Boot Phase 8 / track 15): spring-boot-starter-data-redis
# + @Cacheable makes Redis the cache behind your service with almost no code.
# Redis is single-threaded for commands (so each command is atomic) yet blazing
# fast because everything is in RAM. Use it for cache, sessions, counters,
# leaderboards, rate limiting, queues, and pub/sub — NOT as your primary
# system-of-record for critical data (it's memory-first).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
